# saltext-sqlx rules

Authoritative docs (read before editing code): @BUILD_PROMPT.md @SPEC.md @ARCHITECTURE.md @SECURITY.md @TEST_PLAN.md @TASKS.md

## Always
- Work through `TASKS.md` one task at a time, in order. Stop at the gate and summarise.
- Unit test first for anything under `src/saltext/sqlx/lib/`. Keep `modules/sqlx_mod.py` thin.
- If `SPEC.md` is wrong, fix it in the same change. Record real DB behaviour in `docs/WIRE_SHAPES.md`.

## Never
- Return `[]`, `False`, `None` or a dict for a failure. All failures go through `lib/errors.py` and raise `CommandExecutionError("[E_CODE] ...")`.
- Let a caller supply host, port, driver, URL, `connect_args` or credentials. Profiles only.
- Call `commit(` outside `runner.run_execute`. `query` always rolls back.
- Claim the module enforces read-only. The session guard is bypassable (observed on PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB); the DB role is the control.
- Use `exec_driver_sql` for caller SQL, or f-string a URL (use `URL.create`).
- Write `:x::type` binds (use `CAST(:x AS type)`), or leave literal colons in strings unescaped (`\:`).
- Log or return SQL text, params, rows, URLs or secrets. Mask `*password*` / `*secret*` / `*token*`.
- Add a GPL dependency (`mysqlclient`) or vendor/patch an LGPL one. Ask before adding any dependency.
- Run Python with `src/saltext` as the working directory.

## Remember
- Dialects: postgresql→psycopg, mysql→PyMySQL, oracle→oracledb (thin), mssql→pymssql. Import drivers only in `lib/dialects.py`, lazily.
- One engine per call, `NullPool`, `dispose()` in `finally`. Drop kwargs starting with `__`.
- Hard caps in code: rows 10 000, bytes 8 MiB, timeout 300 s, retry 10 s. Profile/call may only lower them.
- Create PostgreSQL test databases as UTF8.
- Catch `Exception`, not only `DBAPIError`. Take the message from `exc.orig`; never `str()` of a SQLAlchemy exception (it embeds SQL and bound values). Map on the vendor code first.
- On the `salt` command line YAML-quote comparison operators: `operator='">"'` (SPEC section 4). `execute` runs without the session read-only guard; `allow_execute: true` wins over `read_only: true`.
- Oracle and SQL Server are NOT RUN on real servers; do not guess their exception classes or timeout mechanisms (R18).
